WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
I'm on 50th Street in Manhattan and behind me, underneath that scaffolding

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:07.640
is Tad's Broiled Steaks, one of the first fast food restaurants.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.760
I mean, it wasn't quite what we recognize today. It's more of a cafeteria.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.320
But the idea that you went in and you ordered from a very limited menu with limited options,

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:20.200
and it was cheap: that was the brainchild of the Townsend family and Tad's Broiled Steaks.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:21.840
But that's not what I'm here to talk about.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.940
What I'm here to talk about is a slightly less successful idea they had in the 1960s.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:31.180
Here, on the first block of East 42nd Street, about where that McDonald's is now,

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.500
they opened Tad's Varieties.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.520
Now, there isn't much of a record of this. The only contemporary article I could find was in the New Yorker

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.740
in 1963, which explained the concept.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:45.700
You would go in, pick a frozen meal that's been made earlier that day or earlier that week,

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:51.020
take it to a microwave oven on your own table and heat it yourself.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.740
And, well, that's an astonishing idea - that was going to change the world.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:59.740
In fact, the guy who opened the restaurant said that in the future, there would be only two types of restaurants:

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:04.500
Fancy, upscale ones where someone else did the cooking, and cafeterias like that one,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.140
where you went in, picked your frozen meal and set it up in your own microwave oven.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.080
And he was nearly right.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.440
But not quite. He failed to predict home microwaves and the ready-meals, and the TV dinners

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.380
that actually went on to change the world.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:23.620
And the restaurant closed down soon after, but Ray Kroc, the guy who started McDonald's,

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.760
he took advice from the Townsend family.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:30.940
That McDonald's over there is, in some ways, a descendant of a restaurant that might not

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.940
have worked out itself, but still left a bit of a mark on history.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:39.520
Who knows, if history had turned out slightly differently, perhaps there'd be microwave cafeterias

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.520
all over the world.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.100
